🔹 Scenario-Based Git Rebase & Merge Challenges
❓Scenario 6: Splitting a Large Commit into Two
👉 You committed a big change (config + code) as one commit, but your team wants them to be separate.
Solution (Interactive Rebase + Edit):
git rebase -i HEAD~1

   Change pick → edit
   Reset the commit but keep changes:
     git reset HEAD^
   Stage & commit config files:
     git add config/*
	 git commit -m "Update configs"

   Stage & commit code files:
     git add src/*
	 git commit -m "Implement feature X"

Continue rebase:
    git rebase --continue

❓Scenario 7: Aborting a Wrong Rebase
👉 You started rebasing but realized you’re rebasing onto the wrong branch.
Solution:
git rebase --abort

This resets your branch to the state before the rebase started.


❓Scenario 8: Two Developers Push Different Histories
👉 You rebased your branch and force-pushed. Meanwhile, your teammate merged the same branch without rebasing → histories diverge.
Solution:
   Sync with remote:
      git fetch origin
   Rebase on top of remote again:
      git rebase origin/main
   Resolve conflicts, if any.
   Force push safely:
      git push --force-with-lease


❓Scenario 9: Undo a Rebase or Merge Gone Wrong
👉 You messed up during rebase/merge and want to go back.
Solution (Use Reflog):
    git reflog
    git reset --hard <commit-hash>

    git reflog shows all HEAD movements, even across rebases and resets.
    Reset to the commit before the rebase started.

❓Scenario 10: Merge Commit vs Rebase for Release Branch
👉 Team policy: release/* branches must keep merge commits (audit/history), but feature/* branches must be linear.
Answer in Interview:
    For release branches → use git merge to preserve the history of hotfixes and merges.

    For feature branches → use git rebase to keep history clean before merging into main.
